\title{Fitness Landscapes of Mathematical Theories:\\
A Dependency-Adjusted Cost Model and Its Extremal Structure}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{6 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We introduce a cost model for mathematical developments in which a development
is charged for the \emph{transitive closure of its dependencies}, each
declaration paid for exactly once, and rewarded for the number of statements it
proves from a fixed finite corpus. The resulting ratio---\emph{dependency-adjusted
fitness}---turns several folk beliefs about mathematical libraries into precise
theorems. We establish: canonicity of the cost model, via existence and
minimality of transitive dependency closures, the lattice structure of
dependency-closed sets, and the exact identity
$\cost(T\cup U)+\cost(T\cap U)=\cost(T)+\cost(U)$; an ordinal reduction showing
that on a fixed corpus size fitness is the reverse order of cost, hence a finite
maximum principle; a dependency-adjusted global champion theorem, together with
an exact $k$-fold reuse identity quantifying the saving as $k-1$ copies of a
shared core; an adversarial boundary at which canonicity fails while existence
of a minimum-cost cover survives; an exact composition phase transition
(composition pays if and only if the adapter cost is below the shared dependency
mass), in absolute, dimensionless and multiplicative forms; exact candidate
counts interpolating between $2^n$ and $n+1$ as dependency density rises; a
quantitative adapter-valley theorem guaranteeing a relative overshoot
$(\alpha-\beta)/(1+\beta)$ on every semantics-preserving migration across an
abstraction boundary; a style-centre theorem yielding strict local maxima and a
computed three-style metastable landscape; and a no-universal-maximum theorem
showing that raw theorem-per-line fitness is unbounded, with semantically inert
optimisers, in any language admitting conservative inflation at sublinear
marginal source cost.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

Practitioners who build large mathematical libraries hold strong, largely
untested beliefs about the economics of their subject. Abstraction is said to pay for itself.
Merging two libraries is said to be worth it only when they overlap enough.
Migrating a development from one abstraction layer to another is said to get
worse before it gets better. Algebraic, analytic and combinatorial treatments of
the same material are said to persist because each is locally optimal in its own
idiom. And efficiency metrics of the form ``theorems per line'' are said to be
gameable.

Each of these is an assertion about the shape of a landscape whose points are
developments and whose height is some notion of efficiency. This paper fixes a
cost model precise enough to make each assertion a theorem, and then proves the
theorems.

\subsection{The modelling decision}

Everything turns on how cost is charged. Counting only the lines of a
development ignores the tower of prior material it rests on, and so flatters
whichever development leans on the largest tower. Charging naively for that
tower double-counts material used by two parts of the same development, and so
punishes exactly the reuse one wants to measure.

We charge for the \emph{transitive dependency closure}, each declaration exactly
once. This is not one choice among many: Section~\ref{sec:model} shows that the
transitive closure is canonical (it is the \emph{least} dependency-closed
superset of a base) and that dependency-closed sets form a lattice, so that
``the material shared by two developments'' is itself a well-defined body of
mathematics. That is precisely what licenses charging shared dependencies once.

\section{The cost model}\label{sec:model}

\subsection{Declarations, dependencies and closure}

Fix a countable set of \textbf{declarations}, identified with $\N$, together
with a \textbf{source-length function} $\ell:\N\to\N$ and a \textbf{dependency
function} $\deps$ sending each declaration to the finite set of declarations it
directly uses.

\begin{definition}
A finite $S\subseteq\N$ is \emph{dependency-closed} if $\deps(i)\subseteq S$ for
every $i\in S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Lattice property]\label{prop:lattice}
If $S$ and $T$ are dependency-closed, so are $S\cup T$ and $S\cap T$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For the union, $i\in S$ gives $\deps(i)\subseteq S\subseteq S\cup T$, and
symmetrically. For the intersection, $i\in S\cap T$ gives $\deps(i)\subseteq S$
and $\deps(i)\subseteq T$, hence $\deps(i)\subseteq S\cap T$.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:lattice} makes ``the dependencies shared by two
developments'' meaningful as a body of mathematics rather than a mere set of
identifiers.

\begin{definition}
Put $\sigma(S)=S\cup\bigcup_{i\in S}\deps(i)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:step}
$S\subseteq\sigma(S)$; $\sigma(S)=S$ iff $S$ is dependency-closed; and if $T$ is
dependency-closed with $S\subseteq T$ then $\sigma(S)\subseteq T$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The first is by construction. If $\sigma(S)=S$, $i\in S$ and $j\in\deps(i)$,
then $j\in\sigma(S)=S$; conversely closedness makes each added element already
present. For the third, an element of $\sigma(S)$ lies in $S\subseteq T$ or in
$\deps(i)$ for some $i\in S\subseteq T$, hence in $T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
For a finite universe $U$ and base $B\subseteq U$, set
$\cl_U(B)=\sigma^{|U|+1}(B)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Canonicity of the closure]\label{thm:closure}
Let $U$ be dependency-closed and $B\subseteq U$. Then
(i) $B\subseteq\cl_U(B)\subseteq U$;
(ii) $\cl_U(B)$ is dependency-closed;
(iii) if $T$ is dependency-closed with $B\subseteq T$, then $\cl_U(B)\subseteq T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(i) Monotonicity of $\sigma$ gives an increasing chain from $B$; each iterate
stays in $U$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:step} applied to the closed set $U$.
(iii) The same clause applied inductively to $T$ shows every $\sigma^k(B)$ lies
in $T$. (ii) If $\sigma$ did not fix $\sigma^{|U|+1}(B)$, no earlier iterate is a
fixed point either (a fixed point propagates forward), so
$\sigma^0(B)\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq\sigma^{|U|+1}(B)$ is strictly increasing,
forcing $|\sigma^{|U|+1}(B)|\ge|U|+1$ and contradicting
$\sigma^{|U|+1}(B)\subseteq U$.
\end{proof}

Clause (iii) makes the cost model canonical: no modelling freedom remains once
$\deps$ is fixed.

\subsection{Theories, cost, fitness}

\begin{definition}
A \textbf{theory} $T$ consists of a finite set $\cl(T)\subseteq\N$, the
transitive dependency closure of everything it uses, and a finite set
$\pr(T)\subseteq\N$ of corpus statements it proves. Its \textbf{cost} and
\textbf{fitness} are
\[
\cost_\ell(T)=\sum_{i\in\cl(T)}\ell(i),\qquad
\fit_\ell(T)=\frac{|\pr(T)|}{\cost_\ell(T)}\in\Q .
\]
The \textbf{merge} $T\sqcup U$ has closure $\cl(T)\cup\cl(U)$ and proves
$\pr(T)\cup\pr(U)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity]\label{prop:mono}
$\cl(T)\subseteq\cl(U)$ implies $\cost_\ell(T)\le\cost_\ell(U)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact merge accounting]\label{thm:merge}
$\displaystyle \cost_\ell(T\sqcup U)+\!\!\sum_{i\in\cl(T)\cap\cl(U)}\!\!\ell(i)
=\cost_\ell(T)+\cost_\ell(U)$.
In particular $\cost_\ell(T\sqcup U)\le\cost_\ell(T)+\cost_\ell(U)$: merging
never costs more than duplicating, and the saving is exactly the shared
dependency mass $\shared_\ell(T,U)=\sum_{i\in\cl(T)\cap\cl(U)}\ell(i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Inclusion--exclusion for sums of nonnegative weights over finite sets.
\end{proof}

\section{Ordinal reduction and the finite maximum principle}

\begin{theorem}[Fitness is inverse cost]\label{thm:ordinal}
If $|\pr(T)|=|\pr(U)|>0$ and both costs are positive, then
\[
\fit_\ell(T)\le\fit_\ell(U)\iff\cost_\ell(U)\le\cost_\ell(T),
\]
and likewise with $<$ throughout.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With common positive numerator $n$, the map $c\mapsto n/c$ is strictly
decreasing on positive rationals; the strict form follows by negation.
\end{proof}

On a fixed corpus size, nothing about the corpus survives except its
cardinality, and every fitness comparison reduces to one integer measurement.

\begin{theorem}[Finite maximum principle]\label{thm:finmax}
Every nonempty finite family of theories contains a fitness maximiser.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A nonempty finite set of rationals attains its maximum.
\end{proof}

The triviality is the point: existence of a champion is free once the comparison
class is finite. Everything substantive concerns \emph{which} theory it is, and
(Section~\ref{sec:unbounded}) what happens when the class is infinite.

\section{The dependency-adjusted global champion}

\subsection{$k$-fold reuse: an exact identity}

Fix a finite index set $F$ with $|F|=k$, a \textbf{core} $C$, and pairwise
disjoint \textbf{private blocks} $P_i$ ($i\in F$), each disjoint from $C$. Write
$c=\sum_{x\in C}\ell(x)$. The \textbf{shared library} has closure
$C\cup\bigcup_{i}P_i$; the \textbf{specialist} for $i$ has closure $C\cup P_i$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact reuse accounting]\label{thm:reuse}
\[
\cost_\ell(\text{library})+k\,c=\sum_{i\in F}\cost_\ell(\text{specialist}_i)+c .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the disjointness hypotheses,
$\cost_\ell(\text{library})=c+\sum_i\sum_{x\in P_i}\ell(x)$ while
$\sum_i\cost_\ell(\text{specialist}_i)=kc+\sum_i\sum_{x\in P_i}\ell(x)$.
Subtract.
\end{proof}

Equivalently: pooling $k$ specialists saves exactly $k-1$ copies of the core.

\begin{corollary}[Strict domination]
If $k\ge2$ and $c>0$, the library is strictly cheaper than the specialist suite,
hence strictly fitter on a nonempty corpus.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:reuse} plus $(k-1)c>0$, then Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The canonical library is a global champion}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{proof system} $P$ assigns to each declaration its direct dependencies
and to each statement $s$ the finite set $\text{base}_P(s)$ of declarations its
chosen proof consumes. A theory $T$ \emph{proves} $s$ if
$\text{base}_P(s)\subseteq\cl(T)$, and \emph{covers} a corpus $\Gamma$ if it
proves every $s\in\Gamma$. The \emph{canonical library} for $\Gamma$ inside a
universe $U$ has closure $K=\cl_U\big(\bigcup_{s\in\Gamma}\text{base}_P(s)\big)$
and proves $\Gamma$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
The canonical library covers $\Gamma$; and if $T$ is dependency-closed and
covers $\Gamma$, then $K\subseteq\cl(T)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The first is Theorem~\ref{thm:closure}(i). For the second, covering gives
$\bigcup_{s\in\Gamma}\text{base}_P(s)\subseteq\cl(T)$, and minimality
(Theorem~\ref{thm:closure}(iii)) gives $K\subseteq\cl(T)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dependency-adjusted global champion]\label{thm:champion}
Let $\Gamma$ be nonempty and suppose the canonical library has positive cost.
Then for \emph{every} dependency-closed theory $T$ with $\pr(T)=\Gamma$ that
covers $\Gamma$, $\fit_\ell(T)\le\fit_\ell(\text{canonical library})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$K\subseteq\cl(T)$ by the lemma, so the canonical cost is at most $\cost_\ell(T)$
by Proposition~\ref{prop:mono}; both corpora are $\Gamma$, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal} applies.
\end{proof}

The quantified class is unbounded: this is a genuinely global statement, not an
instance of Theorem~\ref{thm:finmax}.

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness up to cost]
Any covering dependency-closed $T$ with $\pr(T)=\Gamma$ whose fitness is at
least the canonical one has exactly the canonical cost.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine the cost inequality of Theorem~\ref{thm:champion} with the reverse one
supplied by Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal}.
\end{proof}

\section{The boundary: alternative proof routes}

\begin{theorem}[No canonical champion with two routes]\label{thm:tworoutes}
There exist theories $R_1,R_2$ and a length function such that both prove the
same one-statement corpus with equal fitness, yet their closures are
incomparable and disjoint.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $\ell\equiv1$, corpus $\{0\}$, $\cl(R_1)=\{1\}$, $\cl(R_2)=\{2\}$. Both
have cost $1$ and fitness $1$; the closures are singletons of distinct elements.
\end{proof}

Thus the family of covering closures loses its least element the instant routes
branch: the intersection of two optimal closures may prove nothing, and the
champion is determined only up to cost. What remains is a weighted set-cover
problem in disguise. Existence, however, survives.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{multi-route} system assigns to each statement a finite set of
alternative routes; a closure proves the statement if it contains one route, and
covers $\Gamma$ if it proves each $s\in\Gamma$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of a minimum-cost cover]\label{thm:mincover}
If a finite universe $U$ covers $\Gamma$, some $c\subseteq U$ covers $\Gamma$
with $\sum_{x\in c}\ell(x)$ minimal among covering subsets of $U$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The covering subsets form a nonempty finite family; minimise an integer over it.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A minimum-cost cover is a fitness maximum among all covering sub-libraries of
$U$ proving $\Gamma$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Composition: an exact phase transition}

Composing two developments pools their closures, saving the shared mass, but
incurs an \textbf{adapter} of source length $A$.

\begin{definition}
$\text{composeCost}=\cost_\ell(T\sqcup U)+A$ and
$\text{dupCost}=\cost_\ell(T)+\cost_\ell(U)$, with fitnesses $\text{cFit}$ and
$\text{dFit}$ obtained by dividing the common numerator
$|\pr(T)\cup\pr(U)|$ by each.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Transition identity]\label{lem:transition}
$\text{composeCost}+\shared_\ell(T,U)=\text{dupCost}+A$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:merge}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact composition threshold]\label{thm:threshold}
Assume the pooled corpus is nonempty and both costs positive. Then
\[
\text{dFit}<\text{cFit}\iff A<\shared_\ell(T,U),\qquad
\text{dFit}=\text{cFit}\iff A=\shared_\ell(T,U),
\]
\[
\text{cFit}<\text{dFit}\iff \shared_\ell(T,U)<A .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal} each fitness comparison is the reverse cost
comparison, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:transition}
$\text{composeCost}-\text{dupCost}=A-\shared_\ell(T,U)$ as integers.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{dependency density} and \emph{adapter density} are
$\rho=\shared_\ell(T,U)/\text{dupCost}$ and $\alpha_A=A/\text{dupCost}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{corollary}[Density form]\label{cor:density}
Composition strictly increases fitness iff $\alpha_A<\rho$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}
Let every declaration have length $10$, let $T$ have closure $\{0,1,2,3\}$
proving $\{0,1\}$ and $U$ have closure $\{2,3,4,5\}$ proving $\{2,3\}$. Then
$\text{dupCost}=80$, the pooled cost is $60$, so $\shared=20$ and $\rho=1/4$.
With $A=10$, fitness rises from $4/80$ to $4/70$; with $A=30$ it falls to
$4/90$; with $A=20$ it is exactly unchanged.
\end{example}

\subsection{Multiplicative candidate populations}

When each result of one theory applies to each result of the other, the
composite proves a \emph{product} corpus; write
$\text{pFit}=|\pr(T)||\pr(U)|/\text{composeCost}$.

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicative criterion]
With $\cost_\ell(T)>0$ and $\text{composeCost}>0$,
$\fit_\ell(T)<\text{pFit}$ iff
$|\pr(T)|\cdot\text{composeCost}<|\pr(T)||\pr(U)|\cost_\ell(T)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicative growth beats additive cost]
If $|\pr(T)|>0$ and
$\cost_\ell(T)+\cost_\ell(U)+A<\cost_\ell(T)\cdot|\pr(U)|$, then
$\fit_\ell(T)<\text{pFit}$ --- composition is profitable whatever the adapter
charge.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\text{composeCost}\le\cost_\ell(T)+\cost_\ell(U)+A$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:merge};
multiply the hypothesis by $|\pr(T)|>0$ and apply the previous theorem.
\end{proof}

\section{Exact candidate counts}

The natural population is the set of \textbf{usable sub-libraries} of a body of
declarations: its dependency-closed subsets. Write $\mathcal{C}(V)$ for this set.

\begin{theorem}[Exact multiplicativity across an independent split]\label{thm:split}
Let $V=A\cup B$ with $A\cap B=\varnothing$ and no dependency crossing the split
($\deps(i)\subseteq A$ for $i\in A$, $\deps(i)\subseteq B$ for $i\in B$). Then
$|\mathcal{C}(A\cup B)|=|\mathcal{C}(A)|\cdot|\mathcal{C}(B)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The maps $S\mapsto(S\cap A,S\cap B)$ and $(S_1,S_2)\mapsto S_1\cup S_2$ are
mutually inverse bijections. Well-definedness of the first: $i\in S\cap A$ gives
$\deps(i)\subseteq S$ and $\deps(i)\subseteq A$, hence $\deps(i)\subseteq S\cap
A$; symmetrically for $B$. The second is Proposition~\ref{prop:lattice}. The
inverse identities use $S\subseteq A\cup B$ and $S_2\cap A=\varnothing=S_1\cap
B$, which follow from disjointness.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If no declaration has dependencies, $|\mathcal{C}(V)|=2^{|V|}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Chain baseline]
If declaration $i$ depends on $i-1$ (and $0$ on nothing) and
$V=\{0,\dots,n-1\}$, then $|\mathcal{C}(V)|=n+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Closedness forces downward closure: by induction on $d$, $i\in S$ implies
$i-d\in S$. Hence a nonempty closed $S$ equals $\{0,\dots,\max S\}$, so the
closed subsets are exactly the initial segments $\{0,\dots,k-1\}$ for
$0\le k\le n$, pairwise distinct by cardinality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $n\ge2$ we have $n+1<2^n$: the maximally dependent library has strictly
fewer usable sub-libraries than the independent one, and the gap is exponential.
Dependency density is exactly the dial between $2^n$ and $n+1$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Valleys and metastable peaks}

For landscape purposes a development is a triple
$(\len,\iface,\content)\in\Q\times\N\times\Q$: source length, principal
abstraction layer, and intrinsic size of the mathematical content. A
\textbf{migration path} of length $n$ is a walk $w(0),\dots,w(n)$ of such
records; it is \emph{semantics preserving} if $\content(w(i))$ is constant.

\subsection{Adapter valleys}

\begin{lemma}[Boundary crossing]\label{lem:crossing}
If $\iface(w(0))\ne\iface(w(n))$, some $i<n$ has $\iface(w(i))\ne\iface(w(i+1))$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Otherwise the interface is constant along the walk by induction, so the
endpoints agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative adapter valley]\label{thm:valley}
Let $w$ be a semantics-preserving migration of length $n$ with content $C>0$ and
let $0\le\beta<\alpha$. Suppose the endpoints use inequivalent interfaces; that
any state crossing the boundary at its next step satisfies
$\len(w(i))\ge(1+\alpha)C$; and that $\len(w(0))\le(1+\beta)C$. Then some
$i\le n$ satisfies
\[
\len(w(i))-m\;\ge\;\frac{\alpha-\beta}{1+\beta}\,m,
\qquad m=\min\{\len(w(0)),\len(w(n))\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:crossing} pick a crossing index $i$, so
$\len(w(i))\ge(1+\alpha)C$, while efficiency gives $m\le(1+\beta)C$. If $m\le0$
the claim is immediate. Otherwise multiplying $m\le(1+\beta)C$ by the
nonnegative factor $(\alpha-\beta)/(1+\beta)$ and using
$\frac{\alpha-\beta}{1+\beta}(1+\beta)C=(\alpha-\beta)C$ gives
$\frac{\alpha-\beta}{1+\beta}m\le(\alpha-\beta)C$. Hence
$\len(w(i))-m\ge(1+\alpha)C-(1+\beta)C=(\alpha-\beta)C\ge
\frac{\alpha-\beta}{1+\beta}m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The guaranteed relative overshoot is strictly positive whenever
$0\le\beta<\alpha$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}
Endpoints of content $100$ at $10\%$ overhead (length $110$) and an intermediate
adapter state at $50\%$ overhead (length $150$) crossing the boundary: here
$\alpha=1/2$, $\beta=1/10$, and the guaranteed overshoot is
$(1/2-1/10)/(11/10)=4/11\approx36\%$; indeed $150-110=40=(4/11)\cdot110$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Three-style metastability}

\begin{definition}
Let $\fit$ be a fitness function, $\adj$ a neighbourhood relation (one bounded
refactoring), and $\style$ a map to methodological styles. A point $b$ is a
\emph{strict local maximum} if $\fit(t)<\fit(b)$ for every neighbour $t\ne b$;
and \emph{style-optimal} if $\fit(t)<\fit(b)$ for every $t\ne b$ with
$\style(t)=\style(b)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Style-centre theorem]\label{thm:style}
If bounded refactorings never change style, every style-optimal development is a
strict local maximum.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A neighbour $t\ne b$ has $\style(t)=\style(b)$, so style-optimality applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quarantine form]
If $b$ is style-optimal and every neighbour of $b$ in a different style is
strictly less fit, then $b$ is a strict local maximum.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Split on whether the neighbour shares $b$'s style.
\end{proof}

This is the realistic hypothesis: boundaries may be crossed, but by
Theorem~\ref{thm:valley} the crossing states carry adapter overhead and are
therefore less fit. The valley and metastability theorems interlock.

\begin{theorem}[Renaming invariance]
Let $\sigma$ be a bijection of developments with $\fit(\sigma x)=\fit(x)$ and
$\adj(\sigma x,\sigma y)\Rightarrow\adj(x,y)$. If $b$ is a strict local maximum,
so is $\sigma b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For a neighbour $t\ne\sigma b$ of $\sigma b$, the point $\sigma^{-1}t$ is a
neighbour of $b$ distinct from $b$, so $\fit(\sigma^{-1}t)<\fit(b)$; invariance
transports this to $\fit(t)<\fit(\sigma b)$.
\end{proof}

Strict local maximality therefore descends to the quotient by
semantics-preserving renaming: the notion is about mathematics, not identifiers.

\begin{theorem}[A metastable three-style landscape]
There is a nine-point landscape with three styles in which each style contains a
strict local maximum, the three maxima have pairwise distinct styles, and two of
the three are not global.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take fitnesses $1,2,5$ (algebraic), $3,7,4$ (analytic), $6,2,9$
(combinatorial), with $\adj$ relating developments of equal style. Each style's
unique maximiser is style-optimal, hence a strict local maximum by
Theorem~\ref{thm:style}: the points of fitness $5$, $7$ and $9$. Their styles
differ by construction, and $5<9$, $7<9$.
\end{proof}

\section{No universal maximum without resource normalisation}
\label{sec:unbounded}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{theory language} $L$ consists of a type of developments, functions
$\cnt,\len:\;L\to\N$, a semantics map $\sem$, a marginal cost $m:\N\to\N$, and a
\emph{conservative inflation} operator $\ext(T,n)$ with
$\cnt(\ext(T,n))=\cnt(T)+n$, $\len(\ext(T,n))=\len(T)+m(n)$,
$\sem(\ext(T,n))=\sem(T)$, and $m$ \emph{sublinear}: for every rational $c>0$
there is $N$ with $m(n)\le cn$ for all $n\ge N$. The \emph{raw fitness} is
$\raw(T)=\cnt(T)/\len(T)$.
\end{definition}

Inflation captures ``state $n$ further consequences of what you already
proved'': the count rises, the semantics does not, and the marginal cost per
consequence tends to $0$ because the schema is written once.

\begin{theorem}[Unboundedness]\label{thm:unbounded}
For any $T_0$ with $\len(T_0)>0$ and any $M\in\Q$ there is $n$ with
$M<\raw(\ext(T_0,n))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Assume $M>0$ (else use $M=1$). Apply sublinearity with rate $c=1/(2M)$ to get
$N$; choose $k>2M\len(T_0)$ and put $n=\max(N,k)+1$. Then $m(n)\le n/(2M)$, so
$M\,m(n)\le n/2$, and
\[
M\len(\ext(T_0,n))=M\len(T_0)+M\,m(n)<\tfrac n2+\tfrac n2=n\le\cnt(T_0)+n
=\cnt(\ext(T_0,n)).
\]
Dividing by the positive length gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No global maximum]
In any such language with a development of positive length there is no $T$ with
$\raw(U)\le\raw(T)$ for all $U$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:unbounded} with $M=\raw(T)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Semantic inertness]
The unbounded family may be chosen so that every witness has exactly the same
semantics as the base development.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Conservativity of $\ext$.
\end{proof}

Raw fitness therefore diverges while the mathematics stands still: the
divergence records no progress whatever.

\begin{theorem}[Normalisation dichotomy]
In any such language, every nonempty finite comparison class contains a
raw-fitness maximum, and the unrestricted class contains none.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[The hypotheses are satisfiable]
Take developments to be pairs (theorem count, length), inflation
$(a,b)\mapsto(a+n,b+\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor)$, and constant semantics.
Sublinearity of $\sqrt{\cdot}$ holds: given $c>0$ choose $k>1/c$ and
$N=k^2+1$; for $n\ge N$ we get $\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor\ge k$, hence
$c\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor>1$ and $\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor\le
c\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor^2\le cn$. This language has no fitness champion.
\end{remark}

A ``global champion'' claim is thus meaningful only relative to a bounded,
normalised comparison class: fixed theorem identity, fixed admissible
dependencies, fixed corpus.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Transitive closure by saturation.} Iterate
$S\mapsto S\cup\bigcup_{i\in S}\deps(i)$ until stable.
Theorem~\ref{thm:closure} guarantees termination within $|U|+1$ rounds and
minimality of the result; a worklist implementation runs in $O(|U|+E)$ time
where $E=\sum_i|\deps(i)|$.

\paragraph{Threshold audit for a merge.} Compute both closures, the intersection
mass, and an estimate $A$ of the adapter length; report $\rho$ and $\alpha_A$.
By Corollary~\ref{cor:density} the merge pays iff $\alpha_A<\rho$.

\paragraph{Minimum-cost cover under alternative routes.} With $r$ routes per
statement, Theorem~\ref{thm:mincover} guarantees a minimiser exists but not that
it is canonical; exhaustive search over route selections is $O(r^{|\Gamma|})$ in
the worst case, so in practice one uses the canonical-closure heuristic and
reports the gap to a lower bound. This is the algorithmic residue of
Theorem~\ref{thm:tworoutes}.

\paragraph{Closed-subset counting.} Exact endpoints are $2^n$ and $n+1$; for
general dependency graphs the count is computable by dynamic programming over a
topological order, or factored using Theorem~\ref{thm:split}.

\section{Applications}

Corollary~\ref{cor:density} turns ``should these two libraries merge?'' into the
comparison of two dimensionless measured numbers. Theorem~\ref{thm:reuse} turns
``how much did that refactor save?'' into an exact count of duplicated core
copies. Theorem~\ref{thm:valley} predicts that any migration across an
abstraction boundary passes through a state at least
$(\alpha-\beta)/(1+\beta)$ larger than the smaller endpoint---so a reviewer who
rejects such an intermediate state as a regression is rejecting the only
available road. The metastability theorems explain persistent methodological
pluralism without appealing to taste. And Section~\ref{sec:unbounded} is a
warning label for any productivity metric of the form theorems-per-line.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The model charges a single additive resource. It does not model proof-checking
time, cognitive load, or value differences among corpus statements---the corpus
is counted, not weighted. Theorem~\ref{thm:ordinal} shows the last to be the
binding simplification: with weighted statements the extremal analysis would
need redoing. Theorem~\ref{thm:champion} is global but relative to a fixed proof
system, and Theorem~\ref{thm:tworoutes} shows this to be sharp. The adapter law
and endpoint-efficiency figures of Theorem~\ref{thm:valley} are hypotheses; the
theorem's contribution is to reduce a qualitative conjecture to two
independently measurable constants.

Open directions include: (i) a \emph{canonicity gap} conjecture---with $r\ge2$
routes per statement, deciding whether a corpus admits a covering library of
dependency-adjusted cost $\le B$ should be NP-complete, with a
$\Theta(\log|\Gamma|)$ worst-case gap between the canonical-closure heuristic
and the optimum; (ii) a \emph{sharp reuse threshold} for subadditive adapter
costs $A(s)\le cs^\theta$, $\theta<1$, under which composing $k$ libraries of
shared density $\rho$ should pay for all $k\ge k_0=O(\rho^{-1/(1-\theta)})$,
with that exponent optimal; (iii) \emph{universality of adapter valleys}, i.e.\
that real corpora satisfy the adapter law with $\alpha\ge1/4$ and
$\beta\le1/10$, forcing an overshoot of at least $3/22$; (iv) empirical
confirmation of the dependency-adjusted champion, of three-style metastability
after quotienting by renaming, and of the multiplicative-reuse phase transition.

Fixing the cost model is enough to settle a surprising amount. What remains open
is not the structure of the landscape but its parameters---and those are
measurements, not theorems.

\end{document}

